{"agent":"perf-sentinel","phase":"core","summary":{"filesAnalysed":5,"findingsCount":1,"blockerCount":0,"estimatedStartupImpactMs":0},"findings":[{"severity":"minor","category":"async","file":"src/pages/_app.tsx","line":111,"codeSnippet":"    const unsubscribe = ContentstackLivePreview.onEntryChange(() => {\n      const cslpElement = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(\"[data-cslp]\");\n      const changedContentTypeUid =\n        cslpElement?.getAttribute(\"data-cslp-content-type-uid\") || contentTypeUid;\n      const changedEntryUid = cslpElement?.getAttribute(\"data-cslp-entry-uid\") || entryUid;\n      ...\n      previewStackFactory\n        .createContentTypeEntryQuery({\n          contentTypeUid: changedContentTypeUid,\n          entryUid: changedEntryUid,\n          lang,\n        })\n        .fetch()\n        .then((result: unknown) => setPreviewPage([result as IContentstackPage]))\n        .catch(() => {});","description":"The onEntryChange callback fires on every CMS edit event (e.g. each keystroke in a rich-text field) with no debounce or throttle, and each invocation issues an outbound Contentstack fetch for the edited entry. Rapid successive edits therefore trigger many redundant fetches with no coalescing; a previous in-flight request is not cancelled when a newer change arrives, so stale results can resolve after newer ones and overwrite previewPage out of order. The .catch(() => {}) also silently swallows all errors, including timeouts.","estimatedImpact":"low","suggestedFix":"Wrap the handler in a short trailing debounce (e.g. 150-300ms) so bursts of edit events coalesce into a single fetch, and/or use an AbortController to cancel the previous in-flight request when a new change arrives. Consider logging rather than fully swallowing errors so preview failures are observable."}],"verdict":"pass"}
